# ENGINE-UNIVERSAL-SERVICES.13 — P2: knot A hand-eliminated at the INNER cycle rule (`ct`).
#
# This is what a generalized `detect_left_recursive_chain_plan` would produce if it did the obvious
# thing — follow the bare-rule-reference chain out of the rule the LINT names first
# (`casting_type`, here `ct`) and rewrite THAT rule to `base ( suffix )*`:
#
#   ct        := ct_base ( ct_suffix )*   ~ casting_type := casting_type_lr_base ( …_lr_suffix )*
#   ct_base   := kw | lit                 ~ the non-cyclic left corners (simple_type, signing, …,
#                                           plus constant_primary shorn of the cycle edge)
#   ct_suffix := "'" "(" lit ")"          ~ constant_cast's residual after its leading casting_type
#
# `prim` and `cast_expr` are UNCHANGED — exactly as they would be, since the plan rewrites only its
# own base rule. The question this probe answers is whether that is enough for the CONSUMER (`prim`,
# i.e. `constant_primary`) to reach the chained text. See README.md.
#
# The entry rule is named `scratch` so this file loads into the scratch slot verbatim.
@entry: true
scratch := prim
prim := lit | cast_expr
cast_expr := ct "'" "(" lit ")"
ct := ct_base ( ct_suffix )*
ct_base := kw | lit
ct_suffix := "'" "(" lit ")"
lit := "n"
kw := "t"
